  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nAs of 07z this morning the surface cold front is located from near \nManhattan KS to southeast Iowa. The main convective complex became \noutflow dominant, but additional mid-level height falls and \nstrengthening low-level jet has allowed more thunderstorm to \nactivity to develop on both sides of the cold front. A few strong \ncores are still possible with elevated CAPE present, the strong \noutflow boundary from late Saturday Night has greatly worked the \nenvironment over and has helped to stabilize much of the boundary \nlayer. While main H5 wave moves through, cyclonic vorticity \nadvection will continue to provide broadscale lift across much of \nthe lower Missouri River through most of Sunday, maintaining rain \nshower chances through most of the day. The main synoptic cold front \nshould push through during the middle of Sunday morning, perhaps \nstill producing stronger convergence in our southern and \nsoutheastern zones through the middle of the afternoon. \nRedevelopment of stronger thunderstorms may be possible from Bates \nCounty MO to Pettis and perhaps even Cooper Counties. But most of \nthe stronger activity should then push toward the Interstate 44 \ncorridor and Ozarks Region, while general shower activity continues \ninto Central Missouri and eastern Kansas. So far rainfall rates have \nbeen fairly light, and should remain this way thus limiting hydro \nand areal flooding issues. We will still continue to monitor river \nlevels and fast responding streams particularly in the KC metro. \n\nBy Monday, the surface cyclone and cold front will have cleared the \nforecast area, leaving seasonable temperatures for the afternoon. \nDeterministic guidance still depicts a closed mid-level mainly over \nthe upper-Midwest, but some lift may still be possible into the \nlower Missouri River Valley. Ensemble suites continue to point to 30-\n40 percent probabilities for light rainfall, especially through \nMonday late morning and early afternoon. Amongst ensemble members \nthough, there is larger spread in how expansive the lift is \nassociated with the feature over the Midwest, and as a result may be \ncausing higher probabilities over too large of an area. This lift \nshould move eastward by Monday Night, where conditions will finally \ndry out. Through the middle of the week, H5 ridge develops over the \nwestern CONUS and provides northwesterly flow, which will help keep \nhigh temperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s. Current forecast \nremains dry through Wednesday, with the next feature that breaks \ndown the mid-level ridge progged to move in Thursday when our next \nmentionable precipitation chances return to the forecast.",
